title: managed instance logs are never rotated (relay-connect log is 810 MiB, +70 MB/day)
updated_at: "2026-09-17T04:38:13Z"
---
## Symptom

`~/.local/share/c2c/instances/relay-connect/log` on xsm is **849,111,601 bytes
(810 MiB)** and growing at ~820 B/s (measured over a 10 s window) = ~70 MB/day.
It has been appended to since **2026-07-15** and has never been rotated or
truncated.

## Root cause

`C2c_relay_managed.spawn_connector` opens the instance log with
`[O_WRONLY; O_CREAT; O_APPEND]` and `redirect_daemon_stdio` does the same for the
supervisor. Nothing ever truncates, rotates, or size-caps it. Every restart
appends to the same file — and with the crash loop that is 9,300 restarts' worth
of output in one file.

`C2C_BROKER_LOG_MAX_BYTES` / `C2C_BROKER_LOG_KEEP` (#61) cover `broker.log` via
`Broker_log.append_json`; managed **instance** logs are not covered by that
mechanism at all. The same applies to every managed instance dir, not just
relay-connect.

## Impact

- Disk consumption grows without bound on any long-lived host.
- Makes the log impractical to inspect with ordinary tools — which directly
  slowed this investigation (every grep had to be run over a `tail -c 200000000`
  slice).
- A crash-looping daemon is exactly the case where the log grows fastest and is
  most needed.

## Fix direction

- Apply a size cap + ring rotation to managed instance logs, reusing the
  `C2C_BROKER_LOG_MAX_BYTES` / `C2C_BROKER_LOG_KEEP` conventions (or new
  `C2C_INSTANCE_LOG_*` vars) so one mechanism covers both.
- Rotate on supervisor start as well as on size, so a restart loop cannot
  concatenate indefinitely.
- Have `c2c doctor` report an oversized instance log.

## Immediate

The xsm log can be truncated once the connector is stopped; do not delete it
while the fd is open or the space will not be reclaimed.

## Evidence

Full investigation: `.collab/findings/2026-09-17T01-50-00Z-kesa-quip-relay-connector-crashloop.md`

Lane: solo (.worktrees/b298-instance-log-rotation). Decision: rotate at supervisor start AND on size via C2C_INSTANCE_LOG_MAX_BYTES / C2C_INSTANCE_LOG_KEEP (defaults 10 MiB / 3). Doctor oversized-log reporting deliberately out of scope here (doctor files are lane-S/B296 territory) — noted as follow-up.